            <ArticleTitle>Primary psoas abscess complicating a normal vaginal delivery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>906-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Psoas abscess is a rare and potentially dangerous complication of normal delivery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">We describe a case of primary psoas abscess after normal vaginal delivery. A young woman presented with fever, left back pain, left lower abdominal pain, and hip pain starting on postpartum day 2. Computed tomography demonstrated a large retroperitoneal collection. Aspiration drainage of the abscess under computed tomography guidance isolated Streptococcus viridans, which responded to antibiotic therapy and percutaneous drainage with complete resolution.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A high index of suspicion is necessary for diagnosis of psoas abscess, which should be considered in postpartum patients with pyrexia, back and hip pain, and a normal neurologic examination. Computed tomography is effective for diagnosis and allows percutaneous drainage of the abscess.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
